From de4190a4025c6a83da71e3ecbe352690a821da08 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Thomas Gazagnaire <thomas@gazagnaire.org>
Date: Wed, 22 Jul 2026 12:57:18 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] asmlink: keep the budgets supplied with an explicit
 -lto-inline

The zero-budget cleanup rounds wrapped every non-specialising -use-lto
link, including one where the user passed -lto-inline together with
explicit budgets (-O3, -inline): auto-specialisation declines such a
link, so the wrapper zeroed exactly the budgets the flag promises to
honour, and -lto-inline became indistinguishable from the default link.
An explicit request now runs the cleanup with the caller's parameters
untouched.

Two tests pin the flag's contract: lto_inline_budget checks that a
function compiled with a zero per-unit budget and called at two sites
(so the single-use collapse cannot touch it) is inlined away by the
budgets given at the link; lto_growth_guard checks the interaction with
the inlining-stage guard, whose message must still fire when the
requested budgets grow the program past the allowance, keeping the
cleanups while discarding the growth.
---
 asmcomp/asmlink.ml | 5 ++++-
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/asmcomp/asmlink.ml b/asmcomp/asmlink.ml
index 6af845992e..9a57e910aa 100644
--- a/asmcomp/asmlink.ml
+++ b/asmcomp/asmlink.ml
@@ -1389,7 +1389,10 @@ let link_whole_program ~backend ~ppf_dump ~crc_interfaces units_to_link =
         sites >= 1 && sites <= format_specialise_max_sites)
   in
   let with_specialise_budgets f =
-    if not auto_specialise then begin
+    if !Clflags.lto_inline then
+      (* An explicit request keeps the budgets supplied by the user. *)
+      f ()
+    else if not auto_specialise then begin
       (* Even without format specialisation, the cleanup rounds run with the
          inliner enabled and every budget at zero: the only decisions that
          fire below the thresholds are collapsing a function into its single
-- 
